{
	title: '/fork: branch a session (Ctrl-B)',
	spec: '/fork opens a new tab right after the current one holding a copy of the current session history up to now (the same copy the spawn tool makes with mode fork, src/host/subagents.ts), same cwd and model and a copy of its unsent draft (unless the draft is a slash command, such as the /fork being submitted), named "<parent name> (fork)" until it names itself, and focuses it. It is an ordinary interactive session: no parent report, no spawn slot. The parent history gets a note that it was forked to <id>; the child starts with the fork note the spawn fork already writes. Key: Ctrl-B (b for branch; Ctrl-F is kept for find). /branch is a hidden alias: it works and completes, but /help and /keys list only /fork. Forking a session that is working copies what is in history so far (a streaming block is not copied half-written) and does not stop the parent. Works the same from the terminal and the web. The fork\'s name is automatic (it may rename itself) and stays within the 60-character name limit: a long parent name is cut before " (fork)".',
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Ordinary forks live in src/host/forks.ts and use the spawn fork history copier without parent/spawn/slots metadata. Focus uses the existing go event, reaching only clients following the parent. The child receives a durable output note even when no copied tool calls need answering; only still-unanswered calls receive synthetic results.',
		'A typed /fork runs inside the submit, before prompts.sent clears the parent draft, so forks.create would copy the literal /fork; it skips drafts that parse as slash commands. Ctrl-B sends a submit without touching the draft, so the parent keeps its draft too.'
